{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012XMUGKoXhF8JS4BL2Dpqv6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MACE-MP-0 paper \"isolated atom\" energies E0 MPtrj spin-polarized oxygen atom energy VASP -1.9 eV\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"MolCryst-MLIPs: A Machine-Learned Interatomic Potentials Database for Molecular Crystals\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.13897\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.12704\"},{\"title\":\"pdf.arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://pdf.arxiv.org/pdf/2401.00096\"},{\"title\":\"mace mp0.md\",\"url\":\"https://tricor.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_sources/algorithms/mace_mp0.md.txt\"},{\"title\":\"github.com\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ACEsuit/mace\"},{\"title\":\"Best practice for calculating E0s (Isolated Atoms) for MACE fine-tuning: Tetrahedron vs. Gaussian Smearing? #1312\",\"url\":\"https://github.fleetingtimer.workers.dev/ACEsuit/mace/discussions/1312\"},{\"title\":\"02 mace mp0\",\"url\":\"https://dongzhaohe321418-lab.github.io/materials-simulation-handbook/ch12-foundation/02-mace-mp0/\"},{\"title\":\"[CP2K-user] [CP2K:21873] Isolated atom energies\",\"url\":\"https://lists.cp2k.org/archives/cp2k-user/2025-October/021935.html\"},{\"title\":\"mace mp 0\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/mace-mp-0\"},{\"title\":\"mace mp 0\",\"url\":\"https://api.emergentmind.com/topics/mace-mp-0\"}]\n\nThe search did not turn up the MACE-MP-0 paper's own isolated-atom table, so I could not confirm the -1.9 eV oxygen value.\n\n- **Closest VASP reference:** A 2026 molecular-crystal MLIP database paper lists a PBE oxygen E0 of -1.531 eV. It computed single atoms in a large box with spin polarization for open-shell atoms like O following Hund's first rule. That is about 0.37 eV higher than your -1.9 eV figure, so the two values depend on setup.\n- **Spin treatment:** A MACE GitHub discussion says the foundation models were trained with spin-polarized isolated-atom data, and that the MPtrj isolated-atom energies were computed by the authors themselves. The MACE is fitted to the atomisation energy, so with the isolated atom energies subtracted. That discussion also reports that an oxygen atom run with tetrahedron smearing gave a positive total energy, which the poster considered unphysical.\n- **Checking -1.9 eV:** The number could come from a different cutoff, smearing, box size, or the MACE-MP-0 paper's own table. To verify it, check the MACE-MP-0 paper's supplementary material or the MACE repository's E0 files for the MP-0 release.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5700ee4c-61b7-45c3-bc83-910d88a8b374","uuid":"4cf51fd7-fef0-409e-ac12-b7d045901910","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:35:35.987Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"MACE-MP-0 paper \"isolated atom\" energies E0 MPtrj spin-polarized oxygen atom energy VASP -1.9 eV","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01CGdFPxfu3hHA9BH7n1MsW8","content":[{"title":"MolCryst-MLIPs: A Machine-Learned Interatomic Potentials Database for Molecular Crystals","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.13897"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.12704"},{"title":"pdf.arxiv.org","url":"https://pdf.arxiv.org/pdf/2401.00096"},{"title":"mace mp0.md","url":"https://tricor.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_sources/algorithms/mace_mp0.md.txt"},{"title":"github.com","url":"https://github.com/ACEsuit/mace"},{"title":"Best practice for calculating E0s (Isolated Atoms) for MACE fine-tuning: Tetrahedron vs. Gaussian Smearing? #1312","url":"https://github.fleetingtimer.workers.dev/ACEsuit/mace/discussions/1312"},{"title":"02 mace mp0","url":"https://dongzhaohe321418-lab.github.io/materials-simulation-handbook/ch12-foundation/02-mace-mp0/"},{"title":"[CP2K-user] [CP2K:21873] Isolated atom energies","url":"https://lists.cp2k.org/archives/cp2k-user/2025-October/021935.html"},{"title":"mace mp 0","url":"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/mace-mp-0"},{"title":"mace mp 0","url":"https://api.emergentmind.com/topics/mace-mp-0"}]},"The search did not turn up the MACE-MP-0 paper's own isolated-atom table, so I could not confirm the -1.9 eV oxygen value.\n\n- **Closest VASP reference:** A 2026 molecular-crystal MLIP database paper lists a PBE oxygen E0 of -1.531 eV. It computed single atoms in a large box with spin polarization for open-shell atoms like O following Hund's first rule. That is about 0.37 eV higher than your -1.9 eV figure, so the two values depend on setup.\n- **Spin treatment:** A MACE GitHub discussion says the foundation models were trained with spin-polarized isolated-atom data, and that the MPtrj isolated-atom energies were computed by the authors themselves. The MACE is fitted to the atomisation energy, so with the isolated atom energies subtracted. That discussion also reports that an oxygen atom run with tetrahedron smearing gave a positive total energy, which the poster considered unphysical.\n- **Checking -1.9 eV:** The number could come from a different cutoff, smearing, box size, or the MACE-MP-0 paper's own table. To verify it, check the MACE-MP-0 paper's supplementary material or the MACE repository's E0 files for the MP-0 release."],"durationSeconds":4.89198701500002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012XMUGKoXhF8JS4BL2Dpqv6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
