{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbpkHbYPwMitNqY1phVw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The adsorption energy of atomic O on Cu(111) is **−4.84 ± 0.3 eV**, at the **fcc hollow** site, relative to an isolated O atom. Both the candidate and the result are on the board.\n\nThe model was **MACE-MPA-0 medium**, not MACE-MP-0 as I said in my earlier update. I relaxed the structures at 0 K on the GPU. The main slab was a 3×3, 4-layer Cu(111) slab (37 atoms with O) at 1/9 monolayer coverage.\n\n| Site | E_ads (eV, vs O atom) |\n|---|---|\n| fcc | **−4.84** |\n| hcp | −4.78 |\n| top | −3.08 |\n| bridge | not a minimum (relaxes into fcc) |\n\n- **Slab-size convergence:** a 4×4 slab gives −4.845 eV and a 5-layer slab gives −4.838 eV, so the numerical error is about 0.01 eV. The 2×2 slab is denser (1/4 monolayer) and gives −4.90 eV.\n- **Against ½ O₂:** the same slabs give −1.69 eV.\n\n**Premise check:** the quantity is well-defined: a 0 K electronic energy with no zero-point correction. One catch is that the model never actually computes the isolated O atom. Its energy is just a fixed per-element constant built into the model (−1.548 eV). That constant only means something if it is a real DFT atom energy. The MACE developers say these constants are spin-polarized PBE atom energies, and an independent PBE calculation gives −1.531 eV, so the reference is usable.\n\n**Validation, with the same setup:**\n- **Cu lattice constant:** 3.640 Å against about 3.615 Å measured, 0.7% high, which is typical for PBE.\n- **Cu cohesive energy:** 3.50 eV against about 3.49 eV measured. This also tests the isolated-atom reference.\n- **O₂ binding energy:** 6.29 eV against about 5.2 eV measured. The model inherits PBE's known overbinding of O₂, which matters directly for an O-atom reference.\n- **Literature DFT:** a web search found one study reporting −5.00 eV (fcc) and −4.91 eV (hcp). I did not open the paper myself. My value is 0.16 eV less binding, and both agree that fcc is the preferred site.\n\nThe 3.5 eV cohesive energy and the ~3.615 Å lattice constant are standard textbook values; the calibration file has no density for Cu.\n\n**Uncertainty:** ±0.3 eV covers:\n- model-versus-DFT error, since surfaces with adsorbates are largely outside its training data;\n- spread between different DFT setups;\n- slab convergence.\n\nIt does not cover PBE's own error against experiment. Judging by the O₂ test, that could mean the real value is several tenths of an eV less binding. I put this in the note rather than as a flag, because the number is reliable as a PBE-level result. I used no measured value referenced to the O atom.\n\nBoard records: candidate `cd358502-e41f-4db3-9894-59ccf874baea`, property_result `d530e42a-5582-4693-8a2f-4f03dc9b1f18`. The calculation script is `/work/scripts/oads.py`.\n\nSources:\n- [Sci China PMA, O on Cu(111) DFT (doi 10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5)](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf)\n- [InterPhon, fcc vs hcp O on Cu(111)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04198)\n- [MolCryst-MLIPs, PBE isolated-atom energies](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.13897)\n- [MACE discussion #1312 on foundation-model atom energies](https://github.fleetingtimer.workers.dev/ACEsuit/mace/discussions/1312)\n- [MACE repository](https://github.com/ACEsuit/mace)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1407,"cache_read_input_tokens":42515,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1407,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5700ee4c-61b7-45c3-bc83-910d88a8b374","uuid":"a41f2019-245e-4ddb-bb86-22501f308862","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:36:26.344Z","request_id":"req_011CgbpkHExdk75ZduUu